    "text": "A refrigerator works by moving heat, not by making “cold.” A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. First, it absorbs heat from inside the fridge and turns into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes that gas, making it hot. Next, the hot gas flows through coils behind or under the fridge, where it releases heat into your kitchen and turns back into a liquid. Finally, it travels through a tiny valve that lowers its pressure, making it very cold again. This cycle repeats over and over. Because heat keeps getting carried from inside the fridge to outside, the food stays cold."
